WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.100
Thanks for joining me on fascinating frontiers, episode 137.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.200
Coming to you on July 20th, 2026.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.340
New week, new discoveries.

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:10.800
Let's see what's happening.

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:14.120
50 years after Viking 1 touched down on Mars,

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.900
new missions are shifting focus from the surface

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.040
to the thin air above the red planet.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:23.460
NASA's Viking 1 mission achieved the first successful landing

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:26.600
on Mars on July 20th, 1976.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.380
Returning the initial surface images from another planet,

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:33.140
the lander operated for over six years

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:36.040
and transmitted data on Martian soil and weather.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.580
Today, the focus has moved upward

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:41.040
with NASA planning atmospheric studies

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.360
that build directly on that early success.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:47.400
The shift tests instruments designed to measure winds, dust,

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:49.900
and trace gases at higher altitudes

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:51.920
than previous surface missions reached.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:54.700
Watch for upcoming orbiter data releases

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.260
that will compare current atmospheric conditions

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:59.060
to Viking era measurements.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:02.140
Now shifting to the next major crude step

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:03.840
beyond low Earth orbit.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.120
Artemis III will coordinate four astronauts

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:09.060
across three separate spacecraft

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.740
over a planned two week orbital stay.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:15.060
The mission requires precise rendezvous timing

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:19.180
between Orion, a lunar lander, and supporting logistics vehicles.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:24.080
This level of integration exceeds any prior NASA crude flight

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:25.720
in the number of moving parts

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:27.580
and real-time decision points.

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.420
The added complexity directly advances

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.440
the tracked Artemis program by testing the full stack

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.900
needed for sustained lunar operations.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.580
Next milestone is hardware integration testing

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:41.260
scheduled for later this year.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.820
On a different note, space X has moved the target date

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:47.180
for the 13th integrated flight test

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.700
to as early as Thursday, July 23.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:55.100
With a 90 minute window opening at 5.45 pm CT from Starbase,

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:57.740
the vehicle remains in final checkout

00:01:57.740 --> 00:01:59.540
after earlier scrub decisions.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:03.400
This update continues the steady cadence of test flights

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.160
that the Starship and Mars program has followed

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:07.580
since last week's coverage.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.500
Rapid iteration on heat shield performance

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.940
and booster catch attempts remains the key open question.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.280
Observers will watch the upcoming static fire

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.120
and rollout sequence for any last minute changes.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:24.000
A Falcon 9 carrying 24 Starlink satellites

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.060
aborted at the final moment during an attempted lift off

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.060
from Vandenberg Space Force Base.

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:32.640
The vehicle and payload stayed safe on the pad

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.000
with no damage reported.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:37.600
This marks the second consecutive last second hold

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.140
for the booster fleet in under 24 hours.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.920
Engineers are reviewing telemetry to isolate the trigger

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:45.220
before the next attempt.

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:47.700
The incident highlights ongoing work

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.420
to maintain high launch cadence

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:51.600
in the commercial space flight program.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.140
Subramanyan Chandrasaykar calculated as a teenager

00:02:56.140 --> 00:03:00.380
that white dwarves cannot exceed roughly 1.4 solar masses

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:01.460
before collapsing.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.480
His insight came during an ocean voyage

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:07.000
and relied on combining quantum mechanics

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.080
with relativity in a way contemporaries had overlooked.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:13.840
The limit still defines the boundary between white dwarves

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:15.220
and neutron stars today.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:19.580
Follow-up papers continue to refine the exact mass threshold

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.520
using new observations of binary systems.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.580
The Chandrasaykar limit story stands out

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:28.540
because it connects a single teenager's ocean voyage

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:31.280
calculation to the fate of entire stars.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:34.860
His work showed that electron degeneracy pressure

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:38.920
can support a white dwarf only up to 1.4 solar masses.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:42.500
Beyond that threshold, collapse to a neutron star

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:44.340
or black hole becomes inevitable.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:48.120
Modern observations of type IA supernovae

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:49.520
still rely on this boundary

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.780
to standardize cosmic distance measurements.

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:55.260
What remains unclear is exactly how rotation

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:58.220
or magnetic fields might nudge the practical limit

00:03:58.220 --> 00:03:59.140
in real stars.

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:02.200
If you pack the mass of our entire sun

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.080
into a sphere the size of Earth,

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:06.380
the material would become so compressed

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:08.600
that normal atomic structure collapses.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:12.480
Electrons are forced into the lowest available energy

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:16.000
states, creating a pressure that no longer depends on temperature.

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:19.380
In a white dwarf, this electron degeneracy

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.260
supports the star against gravity

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:23.620
even after nuclear fusion has ceased.

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:26.400
The process operates at densities

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.680
around a million times greater than water

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:33.000
with surface temperatures that can still exceed 100,000 Kelvin

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.500
shortly after formation.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.640
Yet the same physics sets a hard ceiling.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:41.660
Once the mass climbs past 1.4 solar masses,

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:44.060
the electrons can no longer resist

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:45.780
and the core implodes.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:48.620
We can calculate that threshold precisely,

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:51.040
but we still do not fully understand

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:54.520
how rotation or strong magnetic fields in real stars

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:57.440
might shift the practical boundary by a few percent.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:01.600
The magic magnetomodder aboard the Henon CubeSat

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.420
will sit in deep space to give up to three hours

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:06.680
of advanced notice for solar storms

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:08.680
instead of the current 15 minutes.

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:11.900
The instrument measures magnetic field changes

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:14.840
carried by the solar wind before they reach Earth.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:17.780
Launches planned for early 2027

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:20.940
as part of ESA's Planetary Defense Initiative.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.460
Data will feed directly into space weather models

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:27.240
used by satellite operators and power grids.

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:30.260
Researchers applied machine learning techniques

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:33.200
to large data sets from multiple observatories

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:37.160
to characterize an exoplanet atmosphere in greater detail

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:38.840
than previous methods allowed.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:42.260
The approach combined transit spectroscopy

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:45.440
with orbital dynamics to separate planetary signals

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:48.600
from stellar noise results improve constraints

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:51.640
on atmospheric composition and temperature structure.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:54.200
Further papers using the same pipeline

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:58.520
are expected once additional JWST cycles are processed.

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:01.920
A compact 10-ray imaging system has captured

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:05.060
its initial data from orbit demonstrating a new tool

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:07.260
for monitoring astronaut bone density

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:09.680
and radiation exposure in real time.

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:12.420
The device uses low power detectors

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:15.200
that fit within existing crew quarters constraints.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:18.700
Early images confirm the hardware survived launch

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:20.280
and operates as designed.

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:23.860
Expanded use on future long duration flights

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:25.280
is now under review.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:28.060
High-resolution images show scattered debris

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:30.120
left by a massive dust storm

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.520
that swept across the planet long ago.

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.440
The patterns include windblown ridges

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:37.920
and isolated rock piles that have remained

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:40.120
largely unchanged since the event.

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:42.800
Orbital instruments map the deposits

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:45.480
to reconstruct wind directions at the time.

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:48.860
Continued imaging will track any new changes

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:50.160
from recent storms.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:53.160
A new study documents the steep decline

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:56.420
in cost per kilogram to orbit over six decades,

00:06:57.100 --> 00:07:00.280
driven mainly by reusability and higher flight rates.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:03.360
Projections suggest another comparable drop

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:06.640
could occur by 2040 if current trends hold.

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:09.560
The analysis draws on historical records

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:11.760
from government and commercial providers.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:15.620
Lower costs directly support more frequent robotic

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:18.480
and crude missions across multiple programs.

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:20.840
The lunar phase visible tonight

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:24.960
repeats the exact appearance from July 20th, 1969.

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:28.040
Thanks to the 19-year metonic cycle,

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:30.520
observers can compare current views

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:34.060
with historical photos taken during the first crude landing.

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:37.020
The alignment offers a direct visual link

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:40.040
across more than five decades of lunar exploration.

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:43.780
International discussions on July 20th emphasized

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:45.700
growing amounts of orbital debris

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:48.240
and the need for coordinated removal efforts.

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:51.500
Participants reviewed current tracking capabilities

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:54.980
and proposed new guidelines for end-of-life satellite disposal.

00:07:55.780 --> 00:07:57.980
The session tied debris mitigation directly

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:00.380
to long-term sustainability of lunar

00:08:00.380 --> 00:08:02.020
and cis-lunar operations.

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:05.420
The UN event highlighted risks to satellites

00:08:05.420 --> 00:08:07.240
and future lunar infrastructure.

00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:11.100
Coordinated removal efforts were presented as necessary

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:13.060
to protect expanding operations.

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:15.920
Tracking capabilities were assessed against

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:18.880
the increasing volume of debris already in orbit.

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:21.900
NASA Langley engineers led key elements

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:23.640
of the Viking lander design,

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:27.020
including entry, descent, and landing systems

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:29.080
that succeeded on the first attempt.

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:31.460
Their work established techniques

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:33.820
still referenced in current Mars missions.

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:37.540
Archival documents released for the anniversary detail

00:08:37.540 --> 00:08:39.920
the iterative testing that reduced risk

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:42.020
for the 1976 landings.

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:45.060
Serpens is the only constellation divided

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:47.380
into two non-contiguous parts,

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:50.760
with Ophiuchus separating the head from the tail.

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:54.240
Caput covers more area and contains several

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:56.180
binocular friendly star clusters.

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:59.980
Summer evenings place both sections well-positioned

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:01.400
for northern hemisphere viewers.

00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:04.580
The article reviews the most notable comets

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:07.740
of the century so far, including their orbital paths

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:10.400
and peak brightnesses as seen from Earth.

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:14.040
Nucleus sizes generally stay under 10 kilometers

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:17.400
yet they produce extended tails when heated by the sun.

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:20.500
Observers can use the provided finder charts

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:22.940
for any returning objects this season.

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:27.460
Before we go, keep an eye on the next starship static fire test

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:30.000
for any updates on the booster catch sequence.

00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:32.760
That's fascinating frontiers for today.

